  <p><strong>Can a supplier&#x27;s existing fabric test report be used for a new activewear order?</strong></p>

  <h2>Ask for the pages that identify what was tested</h2>
  <p>Request the full report and trace its specimen back to the fabric or finished garment in the order. If the report only says polyester-elastane, ask for the mill reference, finish and colour used. Confirm whether any wash, stretch or conditioning steps preceded the test. If the tested item cannot be matched, treat the result as background evidence.</p>

  <h2>Match the result to the decision</h2>
  <p>An unsewn swatch can help assess properties of that material. A pocket, seam, print or elastic assembly introduces construction details that the swatch does not represent. Read the actual result, units and agreed limit. If the report only says pass, ask what requirement the laboratory used and who supplied it.</p>

  <h2>Plan targeted retesting when something changes</h2>
  <p>List changes from the tested specimen to the new order, such as mill, finish, colour or garment construction. Ask the lab which checks those changes affect. For example, a new dark colour paired with a light binding can justify a colour-transfer review of the combination. Save the agreed evidence with the purchase order and approved sample so an inspector can trace the decision.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://www.intertek.com/textiles-apparel/textile-testing/">Intertek’s textile testing overview</a>.</p>
